00:01:59.765 --> 00:02:01.809
- I'm a huge fan of long form media.

00:02:01.809 --> 00:02:02.935
- OK.

00:02:02.935 --> 00:02:04.019
- Which is medium to--

00:02:04.019 --> 00:02:05.479
- Like movies?

00:02:05.479 --> 00:02:07.189
- No, mainly on-site so like--

00:02:07.189 --> 00:02:08.482
- [Gary] Got it.
- [Jordan] Vlogs.

00:02:08.482 --> 00:02:10.776
- [Gary] Like when Verge writes a 7,000

00:02:10.776 --> 00:02:12.820
word expose?
- [Jordan] Exactly, 7,000.

00:02:12.820 --> 00:02:13.737
Yeah, yeah.
- [Gary] Yeah OK.

00:02:13.737 --> 00:02:15.698
Vice, big fan.
- [Gary] Got it, OK.

00:02:15.698 --> 00:02:18.325
- [Jordan] What do you
feel is like the next best

00:02:18.325 --> 00:02:20.119
way to monetize off those sites?

00:02:20.119 --> 00:02:23.455
So, beyond sponsored
content, beyond placements,

00:02:23.455 --> 00:02:27.126
how could someone who writes
that make money off of it?

00:02:27.126 --> 00:02:29.003
- You know, I think
that's a good question.

00:02:29.003 --> 00:02:30.921
So you're saying, if you were to start

00:02:30.921 --> 00:02:33.757
a long form media company and you hired

00:02:33.757 --> 00:02:37.511
journalists and your spin was anti,

00:02:37.511 --> 00:02:39.930
let's call it, BuzzFeed, and
it was the other direction,

00:02:39.930 --> 00:02:41.974
and back to the old days of journalism,

00:02:41.974 --> 00:02:43.809
but from a digital standpoint.

00:02:43.809 --> 00:02:46.687
Are you saying, to me the mix of that

00:02:46.687 --> 00:02:48.522
and BuzzFeed has always
been interesting to me,

00:02:48.522 --> 00:02:49.899
are you saying what if I just went

00:02:49.899 --> 00:02:51.400
in that whole direction of just that?

00:02:51.400 --> 00:02:55.654
Like if the site put out
three to four long pieces

00:02:55.654 --> 00:02:57.031
of content a day, is
that where you're going?

00:02:57.031 --> 00:02:57.948
- Yep exactly.

00:02:57.948 --> 00:03:01.243
- You know, to me, obviously
sponsored content would work.

00:03:01.243 --> 00:03:04.163
Obviously people will still sell banners

00:03:04.163 --> 00:03:05.789
and things of that nature
against those things.

00:03:05.789 --> 00:03:07.541
But if you wanted to go
in a different direction,

00:03:07.541 --> 00:03:08.918
there's a couple of places to go.

00:03:08.918 --> 00:03:11.587
First and foremost, I
actually think events

00:03:11.587 --> 00:03:14.048
is a sneaky thing. I don't
think people recognize

00:03:14.048 --> 00:03:17.718
or understand Re/code or
TechCrunch or Mashable,

00:03:17.718 --> 00:03:19.803
how much money they actually make

00:03:19.803 --> 00:03:22.806
on event arbitrage versus ad revenue.

00:03:22.806 --> 00:03:25.184
So I think that's one way to go,

00:03:25.184 --> 00:03:28.270
I also think the keystone
sponsor is something

00:03:28.270 --> 00:03:29.813
that people haven't thought enough about.

00:03:29.813 --> 00:03:32.775
So imagine a world, if
you were the modern day

00:03:32.775 --> 00:03:34.944
Sports Illustrated with long form

00:03:34.944 --> 00:03:38.405
and Pepsi

00:03:38.405 --> 00:03:40.449
was your anchor sponsor.

00:03:40.449 --> 00:03:43.285
Almost like, for anybody who watches ESPN,

00:03:43.285 --> 00:03:46.664
I've been fascinated by 30
for 30, the documentary series

00:03:46.664 --> 00:03:47.998
where the brand comes in and says,

00:03:47.998 --> 00:03:50.084
this 30 for 30 was
brought to you by Levis,

00:03:50.084 --> 00:03:51.543
and then that's it.
(hands smacking)

00:03:51.543 --> 00:03:54.713
I think there's a real play
that if you show quality

00:03:54.713 --> 00:03:56.548
and there's a brand wanting
to be associated with it,

00:03:56.548 --> 00:03:59.301
where there's just one
anchor brand that owns,

00:03:59.301 --> 00:04:02.263
let's call it, like a
stadium naming rights.

00:04:02.263 --> 00:04:03.847
Like if I was starting
something like that,

00:04:03.847 --> 00:04:06.809
I'd go out and try to sell
my thing up front to a huge

00:04:06.809 --> 00:04:10.229
brand for three years as
the anchor sponsor period.

00:04:10.229 --> 00:04:13.107
I actually think television
over the next decade,

00:04:13.107 --> 00:04:16.527
as they start struggling
with monetizing commercials,

00:04:16.527 --> 00:04:18.570
you're gonna see a lot
more product integration,

00:04:18.570 --> 00:04:20.863
but all of us are cynical
to product integration,

00:04:20.863 --> 00:04:23.075
I think if you, for a
lot of you who watch,

00:04:23.075 --> 00:04:24.742
you know how when you watch TV there's

00:04:24.743 --> 00:04:29.373
the NBC or Fox logo, I think
there's a substantial chance

00:04:29.373 --> 00:04:31.125
that in the next decade,
when you're watching

00:04:31.125 --> 00:04:33.127
a TV show, you will see the Fox logo,

00:04:33.127 --> 00:04:35.170
but you'll also see on the other side,

00:04:35.170 --> 00:04:37.673
the Bud Light logo, and they'll be there

00:04:37.673 --> 00:04:40.884
the entire time, and it's
a college show, right?

00:04:40.884 --> 00:04:45.389
So, to me, the keynote, the
kind of the keystone sponsor,

00:04:45.389 --> 00:04:48.600
to me there's doing events around it,

00:04:48.600 --> 00:04:49.852
and so what would that look like?

00:04:49.852 --> 00:04:51.687
It depends on your genre I guess.

00:04:51.687 --> 00:04:54.273
And so those are the
two things that really

00:04:54.273 --> 00:04:56.358
most stand out for me, I also think

00:04:56.358 --> 00:04:58.277
media companies and the
agencies are combining.

00:04:58.277 --> 00:05:00.112
I think one of the weird ways to subsidize

00:05:00.112 --> 00:05:02.531
and make money is, if
your Sports Illustrated's

00:05:02.531 --> 00:05:04.783
playing here, and then
you're producing content

00:05:04.783 --> 00:05:07.536
around sports for brands,
I mean, I do think that

00:05:07.536 --> 00:05:10.664
that is what Vice and BuzzFeed
and Refinery29 are doing.

00:05:10.664 --> 00:05:13.083
And so, that's my answer.
- [Jordan] Cool.

00:05:13.083 --> 00:05:14.043
- [Gary] Thanks, bro.
- [Jordan] Thank you.

00:05:14.043 --> 00:05:14.835
