and for as much in a meeting for

00:10:21.141 --> 00:10:23.268
an hour and a half that I
can talk the entire time

00:10:23.268 --> 00:10:25.354
and nobody else can get a word in,

00:10:26.813 --> 00:10:28.565
I think I listen quite a bit,

00:10:28.565 --> 00:10:30.776
and I listen in a lot of different ways.

00:10:30.776 --> 00:10:33.820
Having a one hour meeting where you listen

00:10:33.820 --> 00:10:37.491
is very different than what
I'm actually doing which is,

00:10:37.491 --> 00:10:39.409
when I actually spend
time with everybody here

00:10:39.409 --> 00:10:43.038
or in the world, that's
actually my time to talk,

00:10:43.038 --> 00:10:45.415
because every other minute, I'm listening,

00:10:45.415 --> 00:10:48.210
every other minute, I'm
looking at what my employees

00:10:48.210 --> 00:10:50.545
are putting on Instagram
from a selfie standpoint.

00:10:50.545 --> 00:10:52.130
(laughter)

00:10:52.130 --> 00:10:54.257
(laughs) You know?

00:10:54.257 --> 00:10:56.468
Every other minute, I'm looking at what

00:10:56.468 --> 00:10:58.553
people are favoriting or consuming,

00:10:58.553 --> 00:11:00.972
or when I walk through
here, looking at what

00:11:00.972 --> 00:11:03.141
they're looking at in
their screen, if they're

00:11:03.141 --> 00:11:05.018
not "working," are they reading Reddit?

00:11:05.018 --> 00:11:06.812
Are they watching YouTube videos?

00:11:06.812 --> 00:11:09.564
The VaynerNation knows this and you've

00:11:09.564 --> 00:11:10.982
watched the show, you know this,

00:11:10.982 --> 00:11:12.818
I'm critiquing the shit out of them.

00:11:12.818 --> 00:11:17.072
Like I'm, again, I think
when it's all said and done,

00:11:17.072 --> 00:11:19.199
I like to think maybe this
is what I'm projecting,

00:11:19.199 --> 00:11:22.077
but I know that I'm doing the reverse of

00:11:22.077 --> 00:11:23.412
what a lot of people think I'm doing.

00:11:23.412 --> 00:11:27.082
And so when I'm with people,
I'm talking a whole lot,

00:11:27.082 --> 00:11:30.544
because it's my time to
take what I've gathered

00:11:30.544 --> 00:11:32.504
throughout every other minute that's

00:11:32.504 --> 00:11:36.591
going on in the world,
to execute again, so I,

00:11:36.591 --> 00:11:38.301
to me it's just a people net game,

00:11:38.301 --> 00:11:42.180
I love being around
people that are like me,

00:11:42.180 --> 00:11:44.850
but the truth is, back
to being an anomaly,

00:11:44.850 --> 00:11:46.685
I don't meet that many people that I think

00:11:46.685 --> 00:11:48.061
are really similar to me and that's

00:11:48.061 --> 00:11:49.354
probably a good thing for the world.

00:11:49.354 --> 00:11:51.064
(laughter)

00:11:51.064 --> 00:11:53.650
I find most people are different than me,

00:11:53.650 --> 00:11:56.111
I definitely feel like, from where

00:11:56.111 --> 00:11:59.823
the market's going, 90%,
95% of the market doesn't

00:11:59.823 --> 00:12:04.202
see or agree with me and I'm
happy that 80% of the time

00:12:04.202 --> 00:12:06.747
I think I'm right on those
things long term, but

00:12:06.747 --> 00:12:09.916
I just like people in general and so to me

00:12:09.916 --> 00:12:13.670
a good solid debate is equally sexy to

00:12:13.670 --> 00:12:16.673
yesing each other and just
glorifying how awesome we are

00:12:16.673 --> 00:12:18.759
or that we see this or
agree on this issue.

00:12:18.759 --> 00:12:21.386
They're weirdly one and the same.

00:12:21.386 --> 00:12:22.387
- Very cool,

00:12:22.387 --> 00:12:23.722
thank you.
- [Gary] Cool, thanks.

00:12:23.722 --> 00:12:25.390
(group applauding)

00:12:25.390 --> 00:12:26.558
Who the, what the, what?

00:12:26.558 --> 00:12:27.517
- [Voiceover] This has
been going on all day.

00:12:27.517 --> 00:12:28.727
- Who's in that room

00:12:28.727 --> 00:12:30.061
and why is it so loud?
- [Voiceover] Suze.

00:12:30.061 --> 00:12:31.104
(thudding)

00:12:31.104 --> 00:12:32.397
(muttering)

00:12:32.397 --> 00:12:33.523
(knocking)

00:12:34.441 --> 00:12:35.442
- No worries.

00:12:35.442 --> 00:12:37.194
(laughter)

00:12:37.194 --> 00:12:39.112
Cool.
- [Voiceover] Alright.

00:12:39.112 --> 00:12:40.280
- (censor bleeping) Loud.

00:12:40.280 --> 00:12:41.239
(laughter)

00:12:41.239 --> 00:12:42.365
- [Voiceover] You can hear everything.

00:12:42.365 --> 00:12:44.868
- I know but, Suze's, projects.

00:12:44.868 --> 00:12:46.787
(Gary sighs)
- [India] Alright, this is

00:12:46.787 --> 00:12:48.246
Geoff, he's from 'Cisco.
- [Gary] (laughs) Gates,

00:12:48.246 --> 00:12:49.289
so good with the shoes.

00:12:49.289 --> 00:12:50.540
(laughter)
- [India] I'm so excited.

00:12:51.374 --> 00:12:52.501
(volume increasing clicking)

00:12:53.752 --> 00:12:56.046
(laughter)
- [Gary] Is he Joe Namath?

00:12:56.046 --> 00:12:59.883
(muttering and laughing)

00:12:59.883 --> 00:13:01.009
Amazing.

00:13:01.009 --> 00:13:03.553
(laughter)

00:13:04.387 --> 00:13:05.931
- Oh hi.

00:13:05.931 --> 00:13:08.099
Gary, I have a question.

00:13:08.099 --> 00:13:11.394
Nationwide CMO recently
justified their Super Bowl ad

00:13:11.394 --> 00:13:14.356
by saying, "We weren't
trying to sell insurance,

00:13:14.356 --> 00:13:16.650
"we were trying to save children's lives."

00:13:16.650 --> 00:13:18.819
How do you feel about advertising

00:13:18.819 --> 00:13:21.196
such a somber spot in the Super Bowl?

00:13:24.241 --> 00:13:26.660
- Great question and great legs Geoff.

00:13:26.660 --> 00:13:29.746
(laughter)

00:13:29.746 --> 00:13:31.206
- You like that, Johnny?

00:13:31.206 --> 00:13:34.626
(laughter and clapping)

00:13:34.626 --> 00:13:38.129
That's a really good question,
it's really interesting,

00:13:38.129 --> 00:13:41.508
I always have wondered if

00:13:41.508 --> 00:13:44.177
everybody talking the next
day after the Super Bowl

00:13:44.177 --> 00:13:46.304
about the monkey that
jumped on the bear with

00:13:46.304 --> 00:13:50.475
the cute little puddle of
water with a smiley face on it,

00:13:50.475 --> 00:13:54.437
did that actually help
XYZ thing sell its thing?

00:13:54.437 --> 00:13:58.316
And I think at some level we have a lot of

00:13:58.316 --> 00:14:02.571
wannabe movie producers
making TV commercials

00:14:02.571 --> 00:14:04.698
for the Super Bowl and it's their chance

00:14:04.698 --> 00:14:06.116
to do the most creative thing that they've

00:14:06.116 --> 00:14:09.035
always wanted to do, and they're so happy.

00:14:09.035 --> 00:14:11.413
I actually weirdly am pretty pro that

00:14:11.413 --> 00:14:13.582
commercial spot, and I'll explain why.

00:14:13.582 --> 00:14:15.750
Number one, at some level,
advertising's supposed to

00:14:15.750 --> 00:14:19.546
make you remember and think and recall,

00:14:19.546 --> 00:14:23.800
and the truth is, that
piece of content makes me

00:14:23.800 --> 00:14:26.303
think about what those people
actually do for a living

00:14:26.303 --> 00:14:28.638
a hell of a lot more than
a lot of other things

00:14:28.638 --> 00:14:31.433
where people bring back
old school celebrities

00:14:31.433 --> 00:14:33.643
and then 10 minutes
later you don't even know

00:14:33.643 --> 00:14:35.896
which snack it really
was or which soda it was

00:14:35.896 --> 00:14:40.442
or which beer it was and
so I understand peoples'

00:14:40.442 --> 00:14:44.070
push back against it,
but I'm a humongous fan

00:14:44.070 --> 00:14:46.990
of going counter-cultural,
and so in a world of

00:14:46.990 --> 00:14:48.992
Super Bowl is festive and this and that,

00:14:48.992 --> 00:14:52.454
and everybody's kind of
going for humor, I mean,

00:14:52.454 --> 00:14:54.289
here we are talking about it, right?

00:14:54.289 --> 00:14:58.001
And so, (sighs) again,
I was at the Super Bowl

00:14:58.001 --> 00:15:00.629
and haven't fully even
seen the spot and don't

00:15:00.629 --> 00:15:02.672
have all the context so I'm
not super prepared to give

00:15:02.672 --> 00:15:04.215
my final, final, final
(hands pounding)

00:15:04.215 --> 00:15:06.927
answer on this, and I'll
try to do that shortly

00:15:06.927 --> 00:15:09.763
because I should be on top
of something like that,

00:15:09.763 --> 00:15:13.600
but my early intuition
is that there's a lot of

00:15:13.600 --> 00:15:16.978
value to always playing the other side of

00:15:16.978 --> 00:15:19.856
the equation and from what I can tell

00:15:19.856 --> 00:15:21.858
without seeing fully the
spot, 'cause I've seen

00:15:21.858 --> 00:15:25.654
the images but not fully
the spot, the content is

00:15:25.654 --> 00:15:29.240
at least closely tied in
to what that company does

00:15:29.240 --> 00:15:32.243
for a living and I think
that way too many people

00:15:32.243 --> 00:15:37.207
wanna throw Betty White out
there or a cute XYZ kitty cat

00:15:37.207 --> 00:15:39.167
or something of that
nature, and you're just

00:15:39.167 --> 00:15:42.128
talking about the
creative, but you have no

00:15:42.128 --> 00:15:44.464
interest in going and buying the product.

00:15:44.464 --> 00:15:48.468
I think there's real
potential for that kind of

00:15:48.468 --> 00:15:50.845
effectiveness so, I think standing out

00:15:50.845 --> 00:15:52.430
and going in the other direction is always

00:15:52.430 --> 00:15:54.474
the right thing in a crowded environment

00:15:54.474 --> 00:15:56.393
and I think they've
clearly done that because

00:15:56.393 --> 00:15:59.604
everybody's still talking
about it and debating it,

00:15:59.604 --> 00:16:01.940
and it's tied into the
business they actually do.

00:16:03.358 --> 00:16:04.818
- [Asker] What's up G?
- [Gary] What's up brother?

00:16:04.818 --> 00:16:06.069
How are ya?
- [Asker] Good, how are you?

00:16:06.069 --> 00:16:08.238
- [Gary] Good to see you, man.
- [Asker] Good to see you.

00:16:08.238 --> 00:16:09.447
What's up everybody?

00:16:09.447 --> 00:16:11.157
I'm Steve Campbell, DRock you can

00:16:11.157 --> 00:16:12.283
(dinging)
link that up right here.

00:16:12.283 --> 00:16:14.285
(laughter)
- Ah.

00:16:14.285 --> 00:16:17.247
I like the right hook,
I like the right hook.

00:16:17.247 --> 00:16:18.999
- Right?
(group applauding)

00:16:18.999 --> 00:16:20.625
I'm a copy writer here at Vayner,

00:16:20.625 --> 00:16:22.961
I've been here about
four and a half years.

00:16:22.961 --> 00:16:24.379
- Oh gee, baby.

00:16:24.379 --> 00:16:25.463
- Oh gee.

00:16:25.463 --> 00:16:26.548
So I've always wanted to ask you--

00:16:26.548 --> 00:16:27.507
- Steelers fan?

00:16:27.507 --> 00:16:29.384
- Steelers fan, gotta get that out there.

00:16:29.384 --> 00:16:31.928
- One of our rare wins
this year for the Jets.

00:16:31.928 --> 00:16:33.680
- Yeah, unfortunately, I remember that.

00:16:33.680 --> 00:16:35.807
(laughter)

00:16:35.807 --> 00:16:40.145
So, I watch you answer
dozens, literally dozens

00:16:40.145 --> 00:16:42.689
of questions over the
years about everything

00:16:42.689 --> 00:16:46.359
from family to business to
wine, I was always curious,

00:16:46.359 --> 00:16:48.820
what is one question that you're shocked

00:16:48.820 --> 00:16:50.530
nobody's ever asked you before?

00:16:50.530 --> 00:16:52.907
- That's a good question, the truth is,

00:16:52.907 --> 00:16:54.826
people have asked me this
exact question before,

00:16:54.826 --> 00:16:56.077
I guess it's never been on film.

00:16:56.077 --> 00:16:59.748
(laughter)

00:16:59.748 --> 00:17:02.917
And since I've answered
thousands of questions,

00:17:02.917 --> 00:17:04.377
not "dozens."

00:17:04.377 --> 00:17:05.670
- Maybe more.

00:17:05.670 --> 00:17:07.756
(laughter)

00:17:07.756 --> 00:17:10.967
- The funny thing is, even though I think

00:17:10.967 --> 00:17:12.676
I get a lot of credit for being able to

00:17:12.676 --> 00:17:14.179
answer questions immediately
(fingers snapping)

00:17:14.179 --> 00:17:15.346
when I don't know what they are and they

00:17:15.346 --> 00:17:17.140
come from so many different directions.

00:17:17.140 --> 00:17:20.435
I'm trying to figure out the
psychology behind this answer,

00:17:20.435 --> 00:17:22.686
it is very intriguing
to me that I don't know

00:17:22.686 --> 00:17:25.857
how to answer this question at all, right?

00:17:25.857 --> 00:17:29.069
I don't know if I'm so pure about

00:17:29.069 --> 00:17:31.946
loving Q and A that
like, I don't even know,

00:17:31.946 --> 00:17:34.657
I'm never prepared for a
question, there's nothing I,

00:17:34.657 --> 00:17:38.453
look, I definitely don't
struggle with telling the world

00:17:38.453 --> 00:17:41.247
things that I wanna say,
so there's no question

00:17:41.247 --> 00:17:43.208
I need for me to be able to communicate

00:17:43.208 --> 00:17:45.043
something that I want to say.

00:17:45.043 --> 00:17:47.253
For a little while there, and
I think the #AskGaryVee show

00:17:47.253 --> 00:17:50.090
has helped it, and definitely
that post I did the other day

00:17:50.090 --> 00:17:52.550
about, "Here's how you
build your personal brand."

00:17:52.550 --> 00:17:54.094
And it was like, "Just shut the fuck up

00:17:54.094 --> 00:17:55.678
"for 10 years and build something."

00:17:55.678 --> 00:18:00.642
I don't think it's that,
it's not that I'm surprised

00:18:00.642 --> 00:18:02.602
nobody's ever asked me questions,

00:18:02.602 --> 00:18:05.897
I do wish that more people asked me

00:18:05.897 --> 00:18:09.400
hardcore business building questions.

00:18:09.400 --> 00:18:11.444
I think that people wanna go towards

00:18:11.444 --> 00:18:14.906
the motivation and the vision
and things of that nature,

00:18:14.906 --> 00:18:19.786
but hardcore tactical, like how
do you staff, against a P&amp;L?

00:18:19.786 --> 00:18:22.622
Like the stuff that I actually
do that’s super nerdy.

00:18:22.622 --> 00:18:25.125
I mean, I'm sure the reason
people don't ask me it

00:18:25.125 --> 00:18:27.502
is because it is super
nerdy, it's not as sexy,

00:18:27.502 --> 00:18:31.506
it's not the sizzle, it's
clearly the boring slab of steak.

00:18:31.506 --> 00:18:34.300
But for me, I take so much pride in being

00:18:34.300 --> 00:18:36.427
an operator, I take enormous
pride in being able to

00:18:36.427 --> 00:18:39.848
build two businesses now,
in a young business career,

00:18:39.848 --> 00:18:44.269
that have both grown from a
small base to 50 million dollars

00:18:44.269 --> 00:18:47.230
in revenue in 36 months, that
just doesn't happen a lot.

00:18:47.230 --> 00:18:50.108
Especially in a non-funded environment,

00:18:50.108 --> 00:18:52.360
neither company got
money to do it, so it was

00:18:52.360 --> 00:18:55.071
all its own growth and using
its own cash flow to do,

00:18:55.071 --> 00:18:58.408
which makes three year growth
so much more difficult.

00:18:58.408 --> 00:19:03.371
So I guess I'm not doing a
good enough job portraying

00:19:03.371 --> 00:19:07.250
that part of my career to
induce that type of audience

00:19:07.250 --> 00:19:09.627
to ask me those kind of questions.

00:19:11.129 --> 00:19:12.130
- [Steve] Cool.

00:19:12.130 --> 00:19:13.214
- Cool.

00:19:13.214 --> 00:19:15.341
(laughter)

00:19:17.010 --> 00:19:19.470
So first and foremost, thank you so much

00:19:19.470 --> 00:19:22.515
for being here guys, thanks
for being part of today's show,

00:19:22.515 --> 00:19:24.851
that was super fun, episode 69 in the bag,

00:19:24.851 --> 00:19:26.144
let's clap it up for yourselves.

00:19:26.144 --> 00:19:31.107
(group cheering and applauding)

00:19:31.107 --> 00:19:34.319
I love when I ask people
to clap for themselves.

00:19:34.319 --> 00:19:36.029
There's something super,
(laughter)

00:19:36.029 --> 00:19:37.739
I don't know, I love it.

00:19:37.739 --> 00:19:39.616
Question of the day,

00:19:39.616 --> 00:19:43.161
what question would you

00:19:43.161 --> 00:19:47.665
really like to ask me based
on Steve's last question

00:19:47.665 --> 00:19:50.627
